 teachers  ben baldwin peter jacoby  james stacy neil mcneil  and bergen evans whose class was so  popular that he filled tech auditorium  year after year i doubt  that professor evans ever knew my name  but he had a profound effect  on my life and my writing all the same  i remember the chess team i captained  we won more often than the football team  but never managed to take down the  university of chicago  as for football i remember when the  student body voted to change the name of  the wildcats  to the purple haze somehow  that didn't stick my years in  evanston were the years that history has  taken to calling  the turbulent sixties there may have  been dances and parties but the  protests were more visible marches  demonstrations strikes rallies sit-ins  love-ins all of it reaching a crescendo  when the student body rose up  against the invasion of cambodia and the  murders at kent state  and tore up the wrought iron fences from  one end of campus to the other  to erect barricades transforming  sheridan road into something akin to the  streets of paris during the french  revolution  for about a week  not long after i found myself standing  with the rest of the class of 1971  where you are standing now diploma in  hand  about to depart medial and begin what my  father called  real life some of my classmates  departed for jobs on newspapers great  and small  others began careers in radio television  and film  joined an advertising agency or the  staff of a magazine  or found employment with uncle sam  over in vietnam the war was still raging  and boys unlucky enough to have had  their birth date pulled in the draft  lottery  found themselves faced with an offer  they could not refuse i got one of those  offers myself  but i had been granted conscientious  objector status  had my application been denied well  no one knows for certain what lies at  the end of roads not taken  but i don't doubt that you can find the  names of some of my classmates  engraved upon 