 vietnam people  could and did  change their positions in the end  that was what the marches and  demonstrations and protests were about  changing minds i was one of those whose  minds  was changed i came to northwestern in  1966  as a patriotic crew-cut high school kid  out of new jersey  convinced that we had to stop communist  aggression  that if we did not fight the vietcong in  vietnam  in a few years we would surely be  fighting them in the streets of bayonne  or evanston it was not any speech  or march or protest that began my  transformation  it was a class assignment a paper i had  to write about the origins of the war  the deeper i delved into the history of  vietnam and the american involvement  there the more  uncomfortable i grew this was not the  story i had been told  the real story did not fit with my  vision of america  and the ideals i thought she stood for i  did not want to accept it  but i had no choice the history was too  convincing  it was the facts that changed my mind  facts a short word a simple concept  truth has a grander ring to it i will  admit but  truth is a tricky thing a lot goes into  deciding  whatsoever things are true given the  same  facts even honest well-meaning and  reasonable men  and women may arrive at different  conclusions  each finding his own truth  but facts are the foundation on which  all truths must rest  unless you get your facts right you have  no hope  of arriving at the truth no doubt  everyone here is familiar with the medal  f some of you may have had a closer  acquaintance with it than you would care  to admit  that term was not in common use back in  the 60s as best i recall the first time  it came to my attention was years after  my graduation  i was certainly familiar with the  concept however  no matter how deft you're writing how  insightful your analysis  how skillful your interview techniques  if you got the facts wrong  even a single fact a single small  inconsequential trivial fact your story  was worthless  readers who noticed your mistake would  lose trust in the whole story  what if the rest of it was wrong too  readers who missed your error  would be misled neither outcome is  acceptable  falsehoods are never acceptable in a  news story whether intentional  or unintentional as mark twain once said  a lie can travel halfway around the  world while the truth  is putting on its shoes and yes  i did get a medal f myself only one  i'm pleased to say and it did not travel  anywhere  it may well be because of that lesson  learned long ago at mcdill  that i am so fearful of where we are  today in this country  in this world i would never have guessed  that the united states  could ever become more divided than it  was in 1971  yet somehow we have achieved that  when i look about today i cannot help  but hear the words of william butler  yates  and turning in the widening gyre the  falcon cannot hear the falconer  things fall apart the center cannot hold  mere anarchy is loosed upon the world  the blood-thin  tide is loosed and everywhere the  ceremony of innocence is drowned  the best like all conviction well the  worst  are full of passionate intensity  there was a time not so long ago when  journalism was  the center of our national life we all  read the same newspapers and magazines  we watched the same television shows  they defined us they united us they were  as much a part of the united states as  baseball  apple pie and the fourth of july and we  trusted them  when walter cronkite would end his  nightly broadcast with  and that's the way it is the country  believed him  liberal readers might not have liked  what they read on the editorial page of  the chicago tribune  conservative readers would bristle at  the editorials of the new york times  but the news on the front page was so  unquestioned  the news was the news the facts we got  from cronkite on cbs  were the same facts that chet huntley  and david brinkley were discussing on  nbc  watergate came a